<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  Merged into the generated iOS Info.plist by Tauri, which looks for this
  filename alongside tauri.conf.json. It lives here rather than in
  gen/apple/*/Info.plist because gen/ is generated and gitignored, so an edit
  made there is lost the next time anyone runs `tauri ios init`.

  NSLocalNetworkUsageDescription is not optional and its absence does not look
  like a permissions problem. Since iOS 14 any connection to a local address
  needs the user's consent, and `read_vpu` opens a plain TCP connection to an
  Analog Way processor on the show LAN. Without this key the system has no
  string to put in the consent prompt, so it never shows one and the connection
  simply fails — surfacing as a connect timeout, which reads as "wrong IP" or
  "processor not responding" and sends you to inspect the network instead of
  the bundle.

  The string is shown verbatim in the prompt, so it says what the app actually
  does with the connection. It reads: the AWJ transport here issues `get` only
  and never writes to the device.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
  <dict>
    <key>NSLocalNetworkUsageDescription</key>
    <string>Aquilon VPU Map reads the screen, layer and VPU configuration from an Analog Way processor on your local network. It only reads — it never changes the processor's configuration.</string>
  </dict>
</plist>
